// Regression fixture for Bug 1: f_replace_program()'s no-op fast path
// (src/packages/core/replace_program.cc) used to silently un-cancel an
// earlier GENUINE pending swap. retrieve_replace_program_entry() implements
// "last call wins" for repeated replace_program() calls within the same
// execution (a second call REUSES and UPDATES the first call's pending
// entry) -- the no-op fast path added to skip pending-swap bookkeeping for
// true no-ops used to return early WITHOUT consulting or updating an
// already-pending entry, breaking that contract: a genuine first call
// followed by a no-op second call left the FIRST call's stale target
// pending instead of honoring the second (last) call.
//
// This shell is a bare passthrough of BASE (identical effective content --
// zero vars/funcs added beyond what BASE itself has), so relative to the
// shell's own (unswapped) current_object->prog:
//   - replace_program(Y), called FIRST, is NOT a no-op: the shell (== BASE)
//     has base_marker(), which Y lacks.
//   - replace_program(BASE), called SECOND, IS a no-op: the shell already
//     == BASE exactly.
// Expected (last-call-wins, matching every other repeated-call case in the
// driver): the object lands on BASE post-sweep, not Y. On the unfixed
// driver, the no-op fast path silently left the first call's stale
// Y-targeting entry pending and the object incorrectly landed on Y instead
// -- discarding what create()'s own last line of code actually asked for.
inherit "/clone/replace_program/replace_bug1_base";

void create() {
  replace_program("/clone/replace_program/replace_bug1_y");
  replace_program("/clone/replace_program/replace_bug1_base");
}
